# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious change, for scanner tests.
# Never run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: trivy-action-teampcp
title: "aquasecurity/trivy-action (TeamPCP): force-pushed tag runs a CI credential stealer"
campaign: teampcp-trivy-2026
date: "2026-03-19"
kind: git-commit
ecosystem: git
name: aquasecurity/trivy-action
version: "0.24.0"
benignVersion: "0.24.0-pre"
summary: >-
  On 2026-03-19 the TeamPCP actor force-pushed about 75 of the 76 version tags
  of the trivy-action GitHub Action (0.0.1 … 0.34.2; only 0.35.0 stayed clean)
  to new, unsigned commits that sat on the current master tree and replaced one
  file, entrypoint.sh, copying each original commit's author, dates and
  message. The injected top of entrypoint.sh collected the CI runner's secrets
  — environment of the runner processes, SSH and cloud and Kubernetes and
  registry credentials, and on hosted runners the referenced secrets scraped
  from the runner worker's memory — encrypted them with a session key wrapped
  by an embedded RSA public key, and POSTed the bundle to a look-alike host,
  falling back to creating a public "tpcp-docs" repo under the caller's token;
  the real scan ran afterwards, so jobs still passed. The same campaign hit
  setup-trivy, poisoned Trivy's own release (v0.69.4) and spread to Checkmarx
  and LiteLLM.
techniques: [ci-compromise, imposter-commit, credential-theft, runner-memory-scrape, openssl-exfiltration, github-repo-exfil-fallback]
reconstruction: >-
  entrypoint.sh follows Socket's and Snyk's description of the injected code:
  the collect loop over the runner processes, the grep for env/ssh material,
  the openssl session-key + RSA-OAEP wrap, the curl POST that checks the HTTP
  code, and the tpcp-docs GitHub fallback — ending in the "## TeamPCP Cloud
  stealer" line the harvester carried. The runner-worker memory scrape is named
  and gated as in the original but is NOT reproduced as working code (a comment
  stands where the /proc/<pid>/mem extractor was). The exfiltration host and
  the GitHub API host are defanged (".invalid"), the embedded key is a freshly
  generated throwaway, and the commit shape (unsigned, one file, an old date on
  a new parent) is what the scanner is meant to notice.
references:
  - https://socket.dev/blog/trivy-under-attack-again-github-actions-compromise
  - https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/security/advisories/GHSA-69fq-xp46-6x23
  - https://www.wiz.io/blog/teampcp-attack-kics-github-action
  - https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/trivy-compromised-a-second-time---malicious-v0-69-4-release
  - https://kudelskisecurity.com/research/investigating-two-variants-of-the-trivy-supply-chain-compromise
indicators:
  - TeamPCP Cloud stealer
  - tpcp-docs
  - scan.aquasecurtiy
commit:
  repository: aquasecurity/trivy-action
  author: Maintainer <maintainer@example.invalid>
  maliciousAuthor: Maintainer <maintainer@example.invalid>
  baseMessage: "Upgrade trivy to v0.53.0 (#369)"
  benignMessage: "docs: note the v0.53.0 bump in the README (#370)"
  maliciousMessage: "Upgrade trivy to v0.53.0 (#369)"
-- shared/action.yaml --
name: 'Aqua Security Trivy'
description: 'Scans container images, file systems and git repositories for vulnerabilities and misconfigurations'
author: 'Aqua Security'
branding:
  icon: 'shield'
  color: 'purple'
inputs:
  scan-type:
    description: 'Scan type, e.g. image, fs, repo'
    required: false
    default: 'image'
  image-ref:
    description: 'Image reference, e.g. alpine:3.20'
    required: false
  format:
    description: 'Output format (table, json, sarif, …)'
    required: false
    default: 'table'
  exit-code:
    description: 'Exit code when findings are found'
    required: false
    default: '0'
  github-pat:
    description: 'GitHub Personal Access Token to authenticate with the GitHub API (avoids rate limits)'
    required: false
runs:
  using: 'docker'
  image: 'Dockerfile'
  entrypoint: '/entrypoint.sh'
-- shared/entrypoint.sh --
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

# Aqua Security Trivy GitHub Action entrypoint. Translates the action inputs
# into a trivy invocation and runs the scan.

TRIVY_VERSION="0.53.0"

scan_type="${INPUT_SCAN_TYPE:-image}"
format="${INPUT_FORMAT:-table}"
exit_code="${INPUT_EXIT_CODE:-0}"

args=("--format" "${format}" "--exit-code" "${exit_code}")
case "${scan_type}" in
  image) target="${INPUT_IMAGE_REF:-}" ;;
  fs|repo) target="${INPUT_SCAN_REF:-.}" ;;
  *) echo "unknown scan-type: ${scan_type}" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

echo "Running trivy ${scan_type} scan on ${target}"
exec trivy "${scan_type}" "${args[@]}" "${target}"
-- shared/README.md --
# trivy-action

A GitHub Action for [Trivy](https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy), the
open-source vulnerability and misconfiguration scanner.

## Usage

```yaml
- uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.24.0
  with:
    image-ref: 'alpine:3.20'
    format: 'sarif'
    output: 'trivy-results.sarif'
```
